      cell-to-cell transmissions.
PG  - 89-117
LID - 10.1080/17513758.2017.1404646 [doi]
AB  - In this paper, a within-host HIV-1 infection model with virus-to-cell and direct 
      cell-to-cell transmission and explicit age-since-infection structure for infected 
      cells is investigated. It is shown that the model demonstrates a global threshold 
      dynamics, fully described by the basic reproduction number. By analysing the 
      corresponding characteristic equations, the local stability of an infection-free 
      steady state and a chronic-infection steady state of the model is established. By 
      using the persistence theory in infinite dimensional system, the uniform 
      persistence of the system is established when the basic reproduction number is 
      greater than unity. By means of suitable Lyapunov functionals and LaSalle's 
      invariance principle, it is shown that if the basic reproduction number is less 
      than unity, the infection-free steady state is globally asymptotically stable; if 
      the basic reproduction number is greater than unity, the chronic-infection steady 
      state is globally asymptotically stable. Numerical simulations are carried out to 
      illustrate the feasibility of the theoretical results.
