LR  - 20210110
IS  - 2050-084X (Electronic)
IS  - 2050-084X (Linking)
VI  - 9
DP  - 2020 Feb 3
TI  - Accelerated viral dynamics in bat cell lines, with implications for zoonotic 
      emergence.
LID - 10.7554/eLife.48401 [doi]
LID - e48401
AB  - Bats host virulent zoonotic viruses without experiencing disease. A mechanistic 
      understanding of the impact of bats' virus hosting capacities, including uniquely 
      constitutive immune pathways, on cellular-scale viral dynamics is needed to 
      elucidate zoonotic emergence. We carried out virus infectivity assays on bat cell 
      lines expressing induced and constitutive immune phenotypes, then developed a 
      theoretical model of our in vitro system, which we fit to empirical data. Best 
      fit models recapitulated expected immune phenotypes for representative cell 
      lines, supporting robust antiviral defenses in bat cells that correlated with 
      higher estimates for within-host viral propagation rates. In general, heightened 
      immune responses limit pathogen-induced cellular morbidity, which can facilitate 
      the establishment of rapidly-propagating persistent infections within-host. 
      Rapidly-transmitting viruses that have evolved with bat immune systems will 
      likely cause enhanced virulence following emergence into secondary hosts with 
      immune systems that diverge from those unique to bats.
