TI  - Global dynamics of a time-delayed nonlocal reaction-diffusion model of 
      within-host viral infections.
PG  - 38
LID - 10.1007/s00285-024-02052-5 [doi]
AB  - In this paper, we study a time-delayed nonlocal reaction-diffusion model of 
      within-host viral infections. We introduce the basic reproduction number R0 and 
      show that the infection-free steady state is globally asymptotically stable when 
      R0 ≤ 1 , while the disease is uniformly persistent when R0 > 1 . In the case 
      where all coefficients and reaction terms are spatially homogeneous, we obtain an 
      explicit formula of R0 and the global attractivity of the positive constant 
      steady state. Numerically, we illustrate the analytical results, conduct 
      sensitivity analysis, and investigate the impact of drugs on curtailing the 
      spread of the viruses.
